Note:
# 1 Chronicles 27 Introduction

## Structure and Formatting

This chapter continues the final section the book, which describes how David established arrangements for the worship of the Israelites. It considers a related matter, how David organized divisions of 24,000 soldiers under specific commanders to serve on a rotating monthly schedule of duty. It then lists the leaders of the tribes of Israel during the reign of David. (Interpreters are not sure why it does not list the leaders of two tribes, Asher and Gad.) It also gives the names of officials who were responsible for various aspects of David’s administration.

In this book, David stresses at several points (22:9, 22:18, 23:25) that the Israelites are now able to build a temple and the Levites are able to assume worship duties because Yahweh has given them “rest” from their enemies, that is, the country is at peace. An organized army and civil administration maintained that peace and supported the worship arrangements that other chapters in this section of the book describe.

1. The rotating divisions of the Israelite army and their commanders (1–15)
2. The leaders of the tribes of Israel during the reign of David (16–22)
3. An explanation of why there is no total of the fighting men of each tribe (23–24)
4. A list of the officials responsible for various aspects of David’s administration (25–34)

### The significance of verses 23–24

Earlier in the book, when the author lists the leaders of tribes, he provides totals of fighting men for some of the tribes (such as in 5:18 and 7:1–11). He seems to feel that readers may expect that in verses 16–22 of this chapter. So in verses 23–24, he refers back to the events of chapter 21 to explain that Joab, on David’s orders, began to count the Israelite fighting men, but he did not finish, because Yahweh punished David and the Israelites for doing this.

### List formatting

This section of the book contains many lists. Review the discussion in the introduction to 1 Chronicles of formatting lists. In your translation, try to present these lists in a way that makes their structure and content clear. For example, the list in 27:16–22 is of the leaders of the tribes of Israel. It begins with an introduction and ends with a conclusion. Its structure and content will be clearer if you present each element on its own line, rather than presenting the entire list as a paragraph or putting each verse on a separate line. For example:

And over the tribes of Israel:
for the Reubenite, a leader, Eliezer, the son of Zichri;
for the Simeonite, Shephatiah, the son of Maacah;
for the Levite, Hashabiah, the son of Kemuel; for Aaron, Zadok;
for Judah, Elihu from the brothers of David;
for Issachar, Omri, the son of Michael;
for Zebulun, Ishmaiah, the son of Obadiah;
for Naphtali, Jerimoth, the son of Azriel;
for the sons of Ephraim, Hoshea, the son of Azaziah;
for the half of the tribe of Manasseh, Joel, the son of Pedaiah;
for the half of Manasseh {in} Gilead, Iddo, the son of Zechariah;
for Benjamin, Jaasiel, the son of Abner;
for Dan, Azarel, the son of Jeroham.
These {were} the commanders of the tribes of Israel.
